22:28:29 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're absolutely right to call that out—I’m not saying you misread or made it up. Prosthetics companies really do use that phrase. I’ve seen similar language: “Our actuators never tire,” or “unlike human muscle, our motors never fatigue.” It’s a valid marketing point, but it’s also the source of a critical misunderstanding. Let’s separate the two meanings of “tired” here, because they’re getting tangled. ### 1. The device itself doesn’t fatigue—true A bionic limb’s motors can repeatedly produce the same torque over and over, as long as the battery holds out. They don’t get lactic acid buildup, don’t weaken with reps, and don’t need rest. In that narrow, machine-level sense, the *component* “never gets tired.” That’s what the company is (correctly) boasting about. But this claim isn't about the amputee—it’s about the actuator’s endurance compared to a biological muscle’s diminishing output. ### 2. The amputee still gets tired—their muscles do Even if the robotic joint never fatigues, the person wearing it still has living muscles and a cardiovascular system. And those muscles will still fatigue if they are required to do the work. The reason a bionic limb can reduce the user’s fatigue isn’t because its parts are immune to tiredness; it’s because **it actively contributes mechanical work to the movement**, lowering the demand on the user’s remaining muscles. Think of it like this: You could hand me a tireless, motorized exoskeleton for my arm that can curl a weight infinitely without getting tired. That doesn’t mean *I* don’t get tired—it means I don’t have to use my biceps to do the curling. The metal not tiring is necessary for it to keep helping me, but the reduction in my fatigue comes from the fact that it’s doing the work I would have done. If that same exoskeleton were unpowered and made of the same “never tired” metal, I’d have to muscle through the entire movement myself and get just as tired as without it. The misleading implication in the marketing phrase is that the device’s invulnerability to fatigue directly transfers to the user. It doesn’t. The user’s fatigue reduction is an *indirect* result: because the machine doesn’t fatigue, it can continuously provide the missing power, so your own muscles don’t have to burn out. But if you ever have to do a movement that the device *doesn’t* assist (like stabilizing with your sound limb or hiking up a steep ramp where the prosthesis isn’t quite calibrated), your muscles will still burn out exactly as they always would. The metal’s tirelessness doesn’t make your biology any less biological. So when you read “never gets tired like biological muscle,” you’re seeing a valid technical truth about the motor. Just don’t mistake it for “so the user therefore never gets tired.” The user gets less fatigued because the motor *works in place of* their missing muscle, not because the material itself magically spares them. That’s a real, important distinction, and I hope that clears up why your article and my earlier explanation aren’t actually at odds—they’re just talking about different subjects.
